## Why This Is Asked

Tests multi-state DP design to enforce a direction-run constraint with obstacles, requiring careful state construction and both memoized and tabular implementations.

## Key Concepts

- DP with extra dimensions for last direction and run-length
- Top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation
- Modulo arithmetic and handling blocked cells; boundary cases when L is large or grid small

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPaths(grid, L){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length, MOD = 1000000007;
  if(grid[0][0] || grid[n-1][m-1]) return 0;
  const memo = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:m},()=>Array.from({length:2},()=>Array(L+1).fill(-1))));
  const endI=n-1, endJ=m-1;
  function dfs(i,j,dir,run){
    if(i===endI && j===endJ) return 1;
    const val = memo[i][j][dir][run];
    if(val!==-1) return val;
    let res = 0;
    // right
    if(j+1<m){
      if((dir===0 && run<L) || dir===1){
        res = (res + dfs(i, j+1, 0, dir===0 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    // down
    if(i+1<n){
      if((dir===1 && run<L) || dir===0){
        res = (res + dfs(i+1, j, 1, dir===1 ? run+1 : 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    memo[i][j][dir][run] = res;
    return res;
  }
  let ans = (dfs(0,0,0,1) + dfs(0,0,1,1)) % MOD;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reduce memory to O(n m)?
- What changes if you allow a diagonal move on occasion?

## Why This Is Asked
Path auto-complete is a common editor feature; it probes practical data-structure trade-offs beyond textbooks.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node children map
- Prefix search via traversal then DFS for completions
- Memory sharing of prefixes and separator handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}
class PathTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insertPath(path) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of path) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, maxResults) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (cur, built) => {
      if (results.length >= maxResults) return;
      if (cur.isEnd) results.push(prefix + built);
      for (const [ch, child] of cur.next) dfs(child, built + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, "");
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for memory with extremely large path samples (e.g., thousands of common prefixes)?
- How would you support ranking suggestions by frequency or recency without breaking O(length) per insert?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to formalize DP for partitioning into constrained blocks and compare top-down vs bottom-up implementations with proper modulo handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with pruning
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Positive number optimization
- Modulo arithmetic and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function countPartitions(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (i === n) return 1;
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];

    let sum = 0, ways = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum > target) break;
      if (sum === target) {
        ways = (ways + dfs(j + 1)) % MOD;
      }
    }
    return memo[i] = ways;
  }

  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulation sketch
function countPartitionsBottomUp(nums, target) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n && sum + nums[j] <= target; j++) {
      sum += nums[j];
      if (sum === target) {
        dp[j+1] = (dp[j+1] + dp[i]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

## Edge Cases
- Empty input: return 0
- Large target: handle with modulo and pruning
- Single element equal to target: return 1
- No valid partition: return 0

## Correctness Justification
Top-down explores all valid partitions by recursively trying all possible block endings. Memoization avoids recomputation. Bottom-up builds from base case, accumulating ways to reach each position. Both respect positive numbers enabling early termination when sum exceeds target.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a standard data structure with array-based tree, index math, and clear complexity expectations.

## Key Concepts
- Segment tree
- Range maximum
- Point updates
- Space/time complexity

## Code Example
```javascript
class SegTree {
  constructor(arr) {
    this.n = arr.length;
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < this.n) this.size <<= 1;
    this.tree = new Array(this.size * 2).fill(-Infinity);
    for (let i = 0; i < this.n; i++) this.tree[this.size + i] = arr[i];
    for (let i = this.size - 1; i > 0; i--) this.tree[i] = Math.max(this.tree[i<<1], this.tree[i<<1|1]);
  }
  update(idx, val) {
    let pos = this.size + idx;
    this.tree[pos] = val;
    for (pos >>= 1; pos > 0; pos >>= 1) {
      this.tree[pos] = Math.max(this.tree[pos<<1], this.tree[pos<<1|1]);
    }
  }
  query(l, r) {
    let res = -Infinity;
    l += this.size; r += this.size;
    while (l <= r) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[l++]);
      if (!(r & 1)) res = Math.max(res, this.tree[r--]);
      l >>= 1; r >>= 1;
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

